Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether $F - F = [0,1]$ (mod 1) with $\mu(F) > 0$ forces $\mu(F) = 1$.

**Answer: No.** I'll construct an explicit counterexample with $\mu(F) = 1/2$.

## Construction

Fix any $0 < \varepsilon < 1/4$ and define
$$F = [0,\, \varepsilon] \;\cup\; \left[\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon,\; 1\right] \;\subset\; [0,1].$$

**Measure:** $\mu(F) = \varepsilon + \bigl(1 - (\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon)\bigr) = \varepsilon + \tfrac{1}{2} - \varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{2}.$

## Computing $F - F \pmod{1}$

We compute all four pieces of the difference set on the circle $\mathbb{T} = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$:

**(i)** $[0,\varepsilon] - [0,\varepsilon] = [-\varepsilon, \varepsilon] \equiv [0,\varepsilon] \cup [1-\varepsilon,\, 1].$

**(ii)** $[\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon,\, 1] - [\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon,\, 1]$: the raw differences range from $(\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon)-1 = -\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon$ to $1-(\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon) = \tfrac{1}{2}-\varepsilon$, giving $[-\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon,\; \tfrac{1}{2}-\varepsilon] \equiv [0,\;\tfrac{1}{2}-\varepsilon] \cup [\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon,\; 1].$

**(iii)** $[0,\varepsilon] - [\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon,\, 1]$: ranges from $0 - 1 = -1$ to $\varepsilon - (\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon) = -\tfrac{1}{2}$, giving $[-1,\, -\tfrac{1}{2}] \equiv [0,\, \tfrac{1}{2}].$

**(iv)** $[\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon,\, 1] - [0,\,\varepsilon]$: ranges from $\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon - \varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{2}$ to $1 - 0 = 1$, giving $[\tfrac{1}{2},\, 1].$

## Taking the union

The cross-terms **(iii)** and **(iv)** alone give
$$[0,\, \tfrac{1}{2}] \;\cup\; [\tfrac{1}{2},\, 1] = [0,\, 1].$$

Therefore $F - F = [0,1]$ (as a subset of the circle), yet $\mu(F) = \tfrac{1}{2} < 1$.

## Conclusion

The implication does **not** hold. The set $F = [0,\varepsilon] \cup [\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon, 1]$ has positive measure $\mu(F) = 1/2$ and satisfies $F - F = [0,1] \pmod{1}$, but $\mu(F) \neq 1$.

